WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.800
Today I want to talk about neutrinos. They’re&nbsp;
fundamental particles, here they are on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.680
standard model of particle physics, and they&nbsp;
are created in the fusion reactions in the Sun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:18.240
which gives out a colossal 10^38 neutrinos every&nbsp;
second, then a massive 10^29 pass right through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:23.760
the Earth every second as if it is not there&nbsp;
and 300 trillion pass right through your body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:30.240
every second, which is why they are often referred&nbsp;
to in mainstream media as ghost particles.
&nbsp;

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.320
Because so many pass through our bodies without&nbsp;
us noticing, we definitely shouldn’t be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:39.120
see them with our eyes, but, I’ve figured&nbsp;
out that there is a very slight chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:43.840
that one of them could hit an atom in your&nbsp;
eyeball and cause a visible flash of light.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:49.600
The chance of this happening to you is very small,&nbsp;
but because there are so many human eyeballs on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:55.280
the planet, roughly 10 people per hour on earth&nbsp;
will have a neutrino collide with one of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:01.120
eyes. Now nothing bad would happen, you’d just&nbsp;
see a very faint flash of blue light, but I’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:05.600
never heard anyone talk about this before even&nbsp;
though according to my calculations, it’s true!
&nbsp;

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.120
Neutrinos are the weirdest&nbsp;
particles in the standard model,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:13.440
and the ones we understand the least. There&nbsp;
are four main reasons why they are weird.
&nbsp;

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:19.600
First of all they are the only particles that only&nbsp;
interact with other particles via the weak force.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:23.840
There are four forces in nature:&nbsp;
gravity, electromagnetism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:25.840
and the nuclear strong and weak forces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:34.160
and the weak force is called weak because it’s&nbsp;
very short ranged, which means that it’s only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:41.120
felt by particles that are really really close&nbsp;
together, like 10^-18m apart - that’s a thousand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:47.600
times smaller than the nucleus of an atom which&nbsp;
is already incredibly small. This is why neutrinos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:51.920
go through your body as if it’s not there&nbsp;
because to them we just look like empty space.
&nbsp;

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:58.960
Technically neutrinos also feel gravity, but they&nbsp;
have such tiny masses and gravity is so weak we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:05.120
can basically ignore it. In fact that’s the second&nbsp;
weird thing about neutrinos, we have no idea where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:11.120
they get their mass from. All the other particles&nbsp;
with mass get their mass from the Higgs field,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:15.200
but neutrinos get theirs from somewhere else,&nbsp;
and we’ve got no idea where they get it from.
&nbsp;

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:22.000
Also, when neutrinos are flying through space&nbsp;
they morph from one kind to another. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:26.480
the only particle in the standard model that&nbsp;
spontaneously changes into another particle.
&nbsp;

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:32.720
And finally we’ve only ever seen left handed&nbsp;
neutrinos and right handed anti-neutrinos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:38.640
All other particles come in both left handed or&nbsp;
right handed kinds of particle and antiparticle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:44.320
but not neutrinos. For more information about&nbsp;
this check out my video and poster on the standard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:49.440
model of particle physics. In fact, in case you&nbsp;
were on the lookout for presents around now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:54.800
I have a load of educational posters available&nbsp;
in my store, dosmaps.com, and I also have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:59.440
range of kids books for the youngsters covering&nbsp;
space, physics, the human body and the oceans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.840
Dosmaps.com link in the description below.
Anyway, like I said, neutrinos are weird and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:10.560
don’t understand them well because they rarely&nbsp;
interact with anything. Despite them being the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:14.880
most abundant particle in the universe: we&nbsp;
think there’s even more neutrinos than there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:23.760
are photons of light. And, even though neutrinos&nbsp;
have incredibly small masses, less than 10^-37 kg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:32.000
the combined mass of all the neutrinos is about&nbsp;
the same as all the stars in the Universe.
&nbsp;

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:37.280
So, to try and understand neutrinos we’ve&nbsp;
built a load of giant detectors underground&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:42.560
to try and detect the very rare occasions when&nbsp;
they do get close enough to collide with matter.
&nbsp;

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:49.040
This is what a neutrino detector looks like:&nbsp;
this one is called Super-Kamiokande and it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:54.400
deep underground to stop any other particles&nbsp;
getting through to it. It’s a giant vat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.040 --> 00:04:01.920
50,000 m^3 of volume, filled with ultrapure&nbsp;
water surrounded by photomultipliers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:07.440
which are basically very sensitive cameras.&nbsp;
Other neutrino detectors follow similar designs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:12.160
but are filled with different kinds of liquid&nbsp;
which absorb different energies of neutrinos.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:17.760
When a neutrino collides with one of the electrons&nbsp;
in the liquid, the electron absorbs their energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:22.720
and wizzes off through the liquid giving out a&nbsp;
special kind of light called cherenkov radiation.
&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:29.440
Cherenkov radiation is kind of like a sonic boom&nbsp;
but for light, because the electrons travel faster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.440 --> 00:04:34.880
than light does in the liquid. Which sounds weird&nbsp;
because it sounds like I’m saying electrons travel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:39.920
faster than light. Well yes they do, but&nbsp;
only because light slows down in a liquid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.640 --> 00:04:44.800
the electrons don’t go faster than light in&nbsp;
a vacuum, that’s the thing that’s impossible.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:48.240
But you can go faster than light&nbsp;
if you slow down light enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:52.320
like in a liquid, and in that case,&nbsp;
you’ll emit this cherenkov radiation.
&nbsp;

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:58.640
So with these massive vats of liquid people are&nbsp;
seeing the sporadic neutrino impacts, collecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:03.920
data and trying to figure out how neutrinos really&nbsp;
work, which is all good science and all that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:10.800
but I was thinking, these detectors are kind of&nbsp;
like big eyes. If you think about it, our eyes are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:17.440
vats of liquid, and your retinas are sensitive to&nbsp;
light like the photomultipliers. So if a neutrino&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:22.880
hit an electron in your eye, would it also create&nbsp;
cherenkov radiation? And could you see it?
&nbsp;

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:29.440
Of course our eyes are way smaller than these&nbsp;
giant detectors, but we have a lot of eyes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:34.880
So I tried to figure it out with maths, and I&nbsp;
also ran it past a professor of these things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:37.600
and yeah, there is a very small&nbsp;
chance a neutrino will collide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:40.240
with your eye gloop and create a flash of light!
&nbsp;

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:48.160
But, this will only happen to you on average once&nbsp;
every 89 thousand years. So you’ll probably never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:55.040
see one, but there are a lot of human eyes on&nbsp;
earth. In fact if you collected all the human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.040 --> 00:06:02.480
eyeballs on Earth right now, you could fill up&nbsp;
Super-Kamiokande, twice over. And that’s why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:09.600
I love physics. Anyway, averaging all this&nbsp;
out over the 7.7 billion people on earth we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:15.200
can expect around ten people per hour will get a&nbsp;
neutrino colliding with one of their eyeballs.
&nbsp;

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:23.840
But, I hear you shout, how visible would this be?&nbsp;
Would we even be able to see it? Well, our eyes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:29.120
are pretty sensitive to light. If you are in a&nbsp;
dark room, you can register flashes of as little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:35.440
as 10 photons, and Cherenkov radiation from&nbsp;
the neutrino impact creates around 200 visible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:43.040
photons per cm through the eye. 200 photons&nbsp;
is way bigger than 10 so a neutrino impact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:47.840
would be visible, but only if you are in the&nbsp;
dark because it would get swamped by daylight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:54.160
Also, there are some subtleties with what&nbsp;
angle the neutrino hits your eye and whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:59.200
the cone of the cherenkov light overlaps&nbsp;
with your retina. I’m not an eyeball expert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:04.560
so I don't really know how that would affect the&nbsp;
statistics. But even if it’s like 10 times lower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:10.800
that’s still one person an hour on earth!
We know astronauts have seen light flashes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:15.840
inside their eyes when they travel outside the&nbsp;
Earth’s magnetosphere, these are thought to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:20.480
caused by cosmic rays, but we don’t get this&nbsp;
on Earth because we are mostly protected by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:25.600
magnetosphere and the atmosphere, but still some&nbsp;
get through so we might get flashes of light in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:32.000
our eyes caused by cosmic rays, or even background&nbsp;
radiation from the Earth. But I think it’s very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:38.640
cool that if you see a random flash of blue light&nbsp;
in your eye, you could be looking at the remnants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:45.120
of the most mysterious particle in the Universe.&nbsp;
Although I should mention that statistically it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:51.920
much more likely to be a detached retina, so if it&nbsp;
keeps happening best to go see an opthamologist.
&nbsp;

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:59.360
Anyway, I’m genuinely interested if any of you&nbsp;
see a faint blue flash in one eye. If we get 90&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:03.920
thousand people to watch this video there’s a&nbsp;
good chance one of you has seen one in the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:09.120
year. So I’m interested to find out, let me know&nbsp;
in the comments below if that’s happened to you.
